### What is reused

Nothing yet. This file exists before the first reuse because the obligation is
easier to discharge with a place to discharge it in than to reconstruct
afterwards, and because the alternative - a first port arriving with its
attribution invented on the spot - is how provenance rot starts.

### Why this file exists, and what the obligation is

NeqSim is licensed under Apache-2.0 and azoth is licensed under AGPL-3.0-or-later.
Apache-2.0 is **one-way compatible** with the GNU licences: Apache-2.0 code may be
incorporated into an AGPL-3.0 work, and the result may be distributed under
AGPL-3.0. The obligation Apache-2.0 imposes is **attribution**, not copyleft
reciprocity - a derivative work must carry the licence, retain the copyright and
attribution notices it received, and state the changes it made. That is what the
sections above and below are for.

Two things make this simpler than it might be. NeqSim ships **no per-file licence
headers** (0 of its 3,371 source files carry one) and **no NOTICE file of its own**,
so there is no downstream notice to reproduce verbatim. And every reuse here is of
an **algorithm**, which is not copyrightable in the first place - what is protected
is the expression, so what is owed is credit and a statement of the differences,
which is what this file gives.

### What is not reused

NeqSim's component databank (`COMP.csv`, `COMP_EXT.csv`) and its binary
interaction table (`INTER.csv`) are **data**, not code, and data is not covered by
the paragraph above - a compilation can carry its own copyright even where the
facts in it do not. If any of it is vendored into this repository, it is entered
here separately with its own provenance, and `docs/src/copyright.md` records the
reasoning.

### Changes

Recorded per calculation in the spec's `ported_from` block, which names the
NeqSim file, the commit, and what was changed. A single global statement of
changes would be less useful than one attached to the algorithm it describes.
